      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Cooper Union, founded in 1859 by industrialist Peter Cooper, sits on Cooper Square and once offered tuition‑free engineering, art, and architecture programs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Village%2C_Manhattan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Phyllis Anderson Theatre became Second Avenue Theater, an annex of CBGB, hosting legends like Bruce Springsteen, Patti Smith, and Talking Heads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Village%2C_Manhattan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1961 the Cooper Union building was inscribed as a National Historic Landmark, recognizing its architectural and educational significance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.responsiblenewyork.com/east-village/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The East Village was a hub for Beatniks, hippies, and punk rock, spawning movements such as Nuyorican literature and punk music.",
